A table of 20,000 rows, rebuilt with an index: one copy and one index
build, whatever the size. (Measured apart: about 0.75 s a million rows.
The rows stay few here because the checks compare every one.)

== before
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;
WITH RECURSIVE n(i) AS (SELECT 1 UNION ALL SELECT i + 1 FROM n WHERE i < 20000)
INSERT INTO dish SELECT i, 'dish ' || i FROM n;

== after
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, note TEXT) STRICT;
CREATE INDEX dish_by_name ON dish (name);

== plan
roux: migrating test.db, 5 steps
  table `dish`: aside, to make it anew
    ALTER TABLE "dish" RENAME TO "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: as schema.sql makes it
    CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, note TEXT) STRICT
  table `dish`: its rows, by id, name
    INSERT INTO "dish" ("id", "name") SELECT "id", "name" FROM "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: the old one
    DROP TABLE "roux_old_dish"
  index `dish_by_name`: as schema.sql makes it
    CREATE INDEX dish_by_name ON dish (name)

== query
SELECT count(*), min(id), max(id) FROM dish

== rows
20000 | 1 | 20000
